 {
  "node_id": "p65_12",
  "index": 12,
  "text": "section 3.2.8.\n32\nAs discussed in the preface, we will sometimes note places where a more modern dialect of C++ provides features\nthat might better express an idea. For example, given that an iterator is a well-known concept having well defined\nsyntactic and semantic properties, the specific C++ type of the iterator becomes unimportant. In such cases we might\nprefer to use the keyword auto to let the compiler figure out that type, rather than force us to type it, or even an alias\nfor it, each time we need it, as the code itself — e.g., lhs.begin() — makes the concept clear:",
